\section{What is printed}{

A \code{parameters} table has one row per parameter: the label column
(\code{Path} when every term is a structural-equation path, else
\code{Predictor}), the estimate (\verb{*Mdn*} for a posterior median, \verb{*M*} for a
posterior mean, \code{Estimate} otherwise), the interval, and then, in this
order, whichever of \verb{*pd*}, \verb{\% in ROPE}, \verb{*BF*~10~} and \verb{*p*} the
table carries. \verb{*R̂*}, \verb{ESS~bulk~} and \verb{ESS~tail~} are added with
\code{stats}. The interval header reads \verb{95\% CrI}, \verb{95\% HDI} or \verb{95\% CI},
from the rows' own \code{ci_method} and \code{ci_level}; rows that disagree on
the level carry it in their cells (\verb{90\% [0.12, 0.48]}), and rows that
disagree on the method carry their label (\code{HDI [0.12, 0.48]}) under
the header \verb{95\% Interval} (\code{Interval} when the levels differ too). A
bare \code{CI} label, which apa7 would format as a column of its own, moves
into the cells the same way, and a \code{ci_label} that apa7 would format
is refused. A \code{diagnostics} table from
\code{\link[=apa_tidy_diagnostics]{apa_tidy_diagnostics()}} has a \code{Term} column and \verb{*R̂*}, \verb{ESS~bulk~}
and \verb{ESS~tail~}.

Every number goes through the format layer (\code{\link[=apa_num]{apa_num()}}, \code{\link[=apa_pd]{apa_pd()}},
\code{\link[=apa_prob]{apa_prob()}}, \code{\link[=apa_bf]{apa_bf()}}, \code{\link[=apa_p]{apa_p()}}) and is decimal-aligned with
\code{\link[apa7:align_chr]{apa7::align_chr()}}, except Bayes factors, whose markup the alignment
would count as digits. A missing value is an empty cell. The ROPE
share drops its \verb{\%}, which the header carries. No header equals a
column name apa7 formats itself, so \code{apa_flextable()} renders the table
as it is.

The note defines the abbreviations the table shows (\verb{*Mdn* = posterior median; CrI = equal-tailed credible interval; *pd* = probability of direction.}), states that the estimates are standardized when every
row is, and on a diagnostics table counts the divergent transitions.
Nothing printed judges the result.

Hypotheses, model comparisons, Bayes factor tables, fit indices,
contrasts and correlations are not tabulated yet: \code{apa_table()}
refuses them by name, and \code{\link[=apa_inline]{apa_inline()}} reports them.
}

\section{Rendering in apaquarto}{

Make the table in an earlier chunk, then name the note in the chunk
option \code{apa-note} of the chunk that renders it:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{```\{r\}
tab <- apa_table(fit)
```

```\{r\}
#| label: tbl-fit
#| tbl-cap: Posterior summary of the regression
#| apa-note: !expr apa_note(tab)
apa7::apa_flextable(tab)
```
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

The two chunks are needed because knitr evaluates an \code{!expr} chunk
option before it runs the chunk, so a table made in the same chunk does
not exist yet when the note is read. \code{apa_note()} refuses a table with
nothing to define rather than return \code{NULL} or \code{""}, because apaquarto
renders either as an empty note, \verb{Note. \{\}}; remove the \code{apa-note}
option for such a table. \code{apa_flextable()} drops the attributes of the
tibble, so the note is read from the tibble, never from the flextable.
}

\section{papaja}{

papaja also exports a function called \code{apa_table()}. Whichever of the
two packages is attached last masks the other's. With both attached,
call \code{apabayes::apa_table()}.
}

\section{The default method}{

On a fitted model or any other object \code{\link[=apa_tidy]{apa_tidy()}} accepts, the default
method calls \code{apa_tidy(x, ...)} and tabulates the result, so \code{...}
takes that route's arguments: \code{standardize = TRUE} on a lavaan or
blavaan fit, \code{effects = "all"} on a brms fit, \code{ci = "hdi"} or \verb{rope =}
on any posterior.
}

